<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Grimble</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1888–1956</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Arthur Grimble</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Grimble was a British colonial administrator who served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> from 1914 and became its resident commissioner in 1926, later governing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a>. He recorded Gilbertese traditions and customs, and his humorous memoir A Pattern of Islands (1952) became one of the best-known accounts of life in a Pacific colony.</p></div>
